{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological natural selection has no conscious agent making a 'preference,' whereas scientific action requires a deliberate choice.","Evolutionary fitness correlates with future survival in a way Popper explicitly denies for scientific corroboration.","The stakes in scientific action (e.g., bridge building) involve immediate human safety, unlike abstract evolutionary models."],"overturn_conditions":["If a formal link can be established between severity of test and increased logical probability of the hypothesis.","If Popper's own writings admit that 'acting on' a theory implicitly assumes uniformity of nature."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on survival of severe tests rather than positive confirmation.","Distinction between retrospective assessment and prospective guarantee.","Rationality defined by adherence to critical method rather than outcome optimization."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_012"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the fittest organism without guaranteeing its survival, we bet on the best-corroborated theory without guaranteeing its truth.","The rationality of the bet lies in the rigor of the test history, not the odds of winning."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive 'critical preference' grounded in the logic of testing rather than probability, but accepting this costs the agent any rational assurance that the preferred theory will succeed in future applications, reducing the preference to a methodological wager without epistemic security.","counterconditions":["If corroboration is shown to logically entail a prediction of future success, the distinction collapses into induction.","If Popper cannot explain why acting on the less-corroborated theory is irrational without invoking probability, the defense fails.","If the 'cost' of no assurance renders the preference practically indistinguishable from random choice for decision-makers, the defense is inert."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'inductive inference' (claiming past success increases future probability) and 'critical preference' (choosing the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification as the best available target for further criticism and action). Preference is justified not because the theory is likely true, but because it is the most informative and rigorously tested survivor, making it the rational choice for a fallibilist who must act despite lacking certainty. The cost is the abandonment of the hope that rationality guarantees practical success; one acts on the best-tested theory knowing it may still fail tomorrow, as corroboration reports only on past performance, not future reliability.","optional_refs":["350739eca4f2195819929272d863a4baaf929266c0b2dcd1506dd8befc5db23e","301c141431fb5df3cd7346d17a2b4a9b2d4b947e5fee6ce5b3b22ef95bef30cd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["350739eca4f2195819929272d863a4baaf929266c0b2dcd1506dd8befc5db23e","301c141431fb5df3cd7346d17a2b4a9b2d4b947e5fee6ce5b3b22ef95bef30cd"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}